WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
After every 30 compressions, you will pause to give two gentle breaths.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Because an infant's body is so small and delicate,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.000
we have to adjust our technique when delivering breaths

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
to ensure we are providing oxygen effectively without causing injury.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Just like with adults and children,

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.000
we start by opening the airway using a head tilt, chin lift.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.000
However, for an infant, you must be extremely gentle.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
You want to tilt the head back just enough to reach a neutral position.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Avoid overextending the neck,

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.000
as this can actually block their narrow airway instead of opening it.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
To form a seal and deliver each breath,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
you will create a seal by covering both their nose and mouth with your mouth.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Do not pinch the nose.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Remember, little lungs need little breaths.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
You are not giving a full breath like you would for an adult.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Instead, deliver a gentle puff of air that lasts about one second.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Your instructor will give each of you a chance to practice this sequence.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Find the neutral head position.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Create a seal over the nose and mouth.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Deliver two gentle puffs, watching for visible chest rise.

